<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slovakia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slovakia is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czechia</a>. From the 11th century until 1918 its lands formed the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, and they then became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Apart from a German client state of 1939–1945, it has been independent only since the peaceful dissolution of Czechoslovakia on 1 January 1993, and it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slovakia.jpg" alt="Slovakia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Ladislav Čisárik and Ladislav Vrtel Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Slovakia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Slovakia covers about 49,000 km2 and is mostly mountainous, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> across most of the northern half. The Tatra Mountains hold 29 peaks above 2,500 metres, and Gerlachovský štít, at 2,655 metres, is the highest point in the country. The largest lowland is the Danubian Lowland in the southwest, followed by the Eastern Slovak Lowland in the southeast. Forests cover 41% of the land, nine national parks cover 6.5%, and hundreds of caves lie under the mountains, five of them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage list. The name means the Land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, and the present Slovak form Slovensko is first attested in 1675.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest human artefacts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> dated to 270,000 BCE, come from near Nové Mesto nad Váhom, and a Neanderthal cranium of about 200,000 BCE was found near Gánovce. Slavs arrived in the 5th and 6th centuries, and parts of the territory came under the Avar Khaganate before the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> was annexed by the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, later Great Moravia. The land passed into the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> in 1000. The Mongol invasion of 1241 and 1242 destroyed much of it, and the south was part of Ottoman provinces in the 16th and 17th centuries.</p><p>The Slovak Uprising of 1848 did not achieve its aim but helped to cement a Slovak national identity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was proclaimed in 1918, with its borders set by the Treaty of Saint Germain in 1919 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon">Treaty of Trianon</a> in 1920. The first Slovak Republic, established in 1939 as a one-party clerical fascist client state under Nazi Germany, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> in 1940. Czechoslovakia became a communist state after the Soviet-backed coup of 1948, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> was suppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion in August 1968, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a> of 1989 ended communist rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Slovakia became independent on 1 January 1993 after the peaceful dissolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and its constitution, ratified on 1 September 1992, took effect that day. It is a parliamentary democratic republic with a directly elected president of limited powers and a 150-seat National Council. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2004, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> in 2007 and the eurozone in 2009.</p><p>The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 83.8% Slovaks and 7.8% Hungarians, the largest minority, and 55.8% of the population identified as Catholic. About 90,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in Slovakia before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and most were murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>; about 2,300 remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slovakia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of graves excavated in Hungary, <b>Slovakia</b>, Austria and Serbia document the culture of this period, especially the cast bronze belt fittings decorated with griffins and tendril patterns that distinguish the Late Avar era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of Bohemia and Moravia, defended <b>Slovakia</b> against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with Poland over Teschen, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Great Moravia was the first major state that was predominantly West Slavic, centred on the Morava River, which gave it its name, in today's eastern Czech Republic and western <b>Slovakia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Basic treaties with Romania, <b>Slovakia</b> and Ukraine renounced territorial claims, though the rights of the roughly five million Hungarians living abroad, mostly in Romania, Slovakia and Serbia, still cause tensions; relations with Serbia grew close, while those with Ukraine deteriorated from 2017 over the Hungarian minority there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The army helped the border guards against illegal crossings from the Eastern bloc frontier until Hungary and <b>Slovakia</b> joined Schengen in 2008.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It is generally held to have been standardised by two Byzantine missionaries from Thessaloniki, Cyril and his brother Methodius, for a mission to Great Moravia, roughly today’s eastern Czech Republic and western <b>Slovakia</b>, sent in answer to a request by its ruler, Duke Rastislav, for a Slavonic liturgy.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-church-slavonic">Old Church Slavonic · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
